Jove wrote:
>
>   Skills must be exercised to be #enhanceable.  Weapons only count
> as exercised if at least 2(?) points of damage are done.  (Some
> threshold, anyway.)
>
>   To improve the chances of improving skill with daggers:
>
>   - Try to practice with dagger(s) with a positive enchantment.

Or any of the 3 artifacts in the dagger class.  One only has doubled
damage against orcs, but early on that tends to be good enough.

>   - Elven daggers are better than crude/plain daggers in that
>     elven daggers are rust/corrode/fire/whatever proof.

Being rustproof makes them quite valuable against certain monsters.
Rust monsters in particular, but there are two types of pudding
where one causes rust damage and the other rot damage.

> Elven
>     daggers are also rarer than crude/plain daggers, so collecting
>     a minimal stack will take longer.

They are carried by elves (duh) and sometimes also by hobbits,
so after you've explored long enough you will definitely end up
with a nice big stack.

But crude orcish daggers are carried by orcs (duh) and they
appear early in the dungeon.  You'll end up with a stack of
them.  First build up a stock of at least ten on any type of
daggers.  Then gradually ditch crude one by one as regular
daggers appear and then gradually ditch the regular ones
as elvish appear.

>   - Silver daggers are more stylish yet, but are incredibly rare.
>     Extensive polymorph sessions are generally necessary to get
>     a decent stack of silver daggers.

Silver is immune to all damage but that doesn't make a
silver dagger worth a wish in most games.  Athame is also
in the dagger class and they have other good uses.

>   For wizards, a stack of highly enchanted daggers is a nice
> last resort for tough monsters with magic resistance or reflection.
> Your best attack spells (magic missile, finger of death) won't
> do any good.  But nothing is immune to physical attack by highly
> enchanted weapons.

Since wizards first gift is an athame in the dagger class they
can start building towards a skill #advance very on.  And
daggers are available very early in the game.  It makes sense to
play a wizard who alternates between attack spells and thrown
daggers who only uses the big stick in emergencies.



